---
import '../styles/global.css';
import Gates from '../components/Gates.astro';
import EffectPlot from '../components/EffectPlot.astro';
import {results,artifact,release,paper,source,number,bibtex} from '../data/results';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const title='COHE: Auditing Non-Transitivity in Sample Difficulty Proxies for Vision Models';
const description='When does a sample difficulty proxy actually help? COHE audits dependence, held-out prediction, and protocol-specific operational transfer. NeurIPS 2026 Evaluations & Datasets.';
const canonical=Astro.site?new URL(base,Astro.site).href:undefined;
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/><title>{title} · NeurIPS 2026</title><meta name="description" content={description}/><meta name="theme-color" content="#faf9f6"/><link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}favicon.svg`}/>{canonical&&<link rel="canonical" href={canonical}/>}<meta property="og:type" content="website"/><meta property="og:title" content={title}/><meta property="og:description" content={description}/><meta property="og:site_name" content="COHE · NeurIPS 2026"/>{canonical&&<meta property="og:url" content={canonical}/>}<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary"/><meta name="twitter:title" content={title}/><meta name="twitter:description" content={description}/></head>
<body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header container"><a class="wordmark" href="#" aria-label="COHE home">COHE<span class="wordmark-rule"></span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#framework">Framework</a><a href="#findings">Findings</a><a href="#resources">Resources</a></nav><span class="header-venue">NeurIPS ’26</span></header>
<main id="main"><section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="paper-title"><div class="hero-meta"><span>NeurIPS 2026</span><span>Evaluations &amp; Datasets Track</span><span class="accepted">Accepted paper</span></div><div class="hero-layout"><div class="hero-copy"><h1 id="paper-title"><span class="hero-acronym">COHE<span class="title-colon">:</span></span><span class="hero-title">Auditing Non-Transitivity in Sample Difficulty Proxies for Vision Models</span></h1><p class="hero-deck">A proxy can predict difficulty.<br/>That alone doesn’t make it useful.</p><div class="authors"><span>Dayena Jeong<sup>1</sup></span><span>Sunglok Choi<sup>2</sup></span></div><p class="university">Seoul National University of Science and Technology</p><div class="affiliations"><p><sup>1</sup> Department of Defense Artificial Intelligence Applications</p><p><sup>2</sup> Department of Computer Science and Engineering</p></div><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href={paper}>Paper</a><a class="button" href={artifact}>Code</a><a class="button" href={release}>Checkpoints</a><a class="button" href="#citation">BibTeX</a></div></div><figure class="hero-diagram"><div class="diagram-heading"><span class="overline">The inference gap</span><span class="diagram-id">FIG. 00</span></div><div class="inference-row"><span class="mini-label">A score</span><strong>Proxy<br/>difficulty</strong><span class="diagram-description">Reconstruction, denoising,<br/>likelihood, or isolation</span></div><div class="gap"><span>≠</span><small>does not establish</small></div><div class="inference-row"><span class="mini-label">A target</span><strong>Discriminative<br/>difficulty</strong><span class="diagram-description">A chosen classifier<br/>and target definition</span></div><div class="gap"><span>≠</span><small>does not establish</small></div><div class="inference-row final-inference"><span class="mini-label">A decision</span><strong>Downstream<br/>utility</strong><span class="diagram-description">Benefit under the<br/>declared protocol</span></div><figcaption>Conceptual illustration — not experimental data. These are distinct questions, not numerical inequalities.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hero-bottom"><span>Cross-Objective Hardness Evaluation</span><a href="#motivation">Explore the study <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></section>
<section id="motivation" class="motivation container section" aria-labelledby="motivation-title"><span class="section-number">01 / The question</span><div><h2 id="motivation-title">When does a sample difficulty<br class="desktop-break"/> proxy <em>actually help?</em></h2><p class="lead">An image may be hard to reconstruct, hard to classify, or useful to select for training. Evidence for one does not automatically establish the others.</p><p>COHE is an evaluation and auditing framework. It asks which downstream claim the evidence supports, for a specified proxy, target, and protocol.</p></div></section>
<Gates/>
<section id="findings" class="findings container section" aria-labelledby="findings-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">03 / Experimental findings</span><h2 id="findings-title">The gap shows up<br/><em>in the experiments.</em></h2><p>Three operational case studies. Each comparison stays within its declared policy and learner.</p></div>
<article class="case-study"><div class="case-copy"><span class="overline">01 · DINOv2 / CIFAR-100</span><h3>Prediction survives.<br/> Selection does not.</h3><p>Isolation relates to first-learning (ρ = {number(results.firstLearning.rho,4)}, R² = {number(results.firstLearning.r2,4)}), yet direct hard-only selection underperforms Random in all four conditions.</p><p class="scope">ResNet-18 · five paired seeds per condition. All 20 paired differences are negative. Balanced selection uses a balanced Random baseline.</p><p class="case-limit">The first-learning relation remains meaningful. Other curricula, weighting, architectures, and tasks are not established by these tests.</p><a class="source-link" href={source(results.sources.dino)}>Seed records &amp; provenance</a></div><div class="case-figure"><EffectPlot rows={results.dino} min={-20} max={0} ticks={[-20,-15,-10,-5,0]} label="DINOv2 hard-selection differences versus matched Random"/><details class="seed-details"><summary>Mean accuracy and matched baselines</summary><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>DINOv2, Top-1 accuracy (%)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Selection</th><th scope="col">Hard</th><th scope="col">Random</th></tr></thead><tbody>{results.dino.map(r=><tr><th scope="row">{r.label}</th><td>{number(r.hardMean,2)}</td><td>{number(r.randomMean,2)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details></div></article>
<article class="case-study"><div class="case-copy"><span class="overline">02 · DDPM / CIFAR-100</span><h3>Blocked ordering<br/> does not improve accuracy.</h3><p>Easy-to-Hard and Shuffled-Proxy Order both fall below global Random ordering. Their difference is not statistically established.</p><p class="scope">ResNet-18 · eight paired seeds · 200 epochs. All 50,000 samples appear once per epoch, using ten fixed bins and matched step counts.</p><p class="case-limit">Easy − Shuffled: {number(results.ddpm[2].delta)} pp, 95% CI [{number(results.ddpm[2].ci[0])}, {number(results.ddpm[2].ci[1])}]. Harm cannot be uniquely attributed to true score–sample correspondence.</p><a class="source-link" href={source(results.sources.ddpm)}>Seed records &amp; provenance</a></div><div class="case-figure"><EffectPlot rows={results.ddpm.slice(0,2)} min={-1.4} max={0.4} ticks={[-1,-0.5,0]} label="DDPM blocked-ordering differences versus global Random"/><div class="comparison-note"><span class="overline">The correspondence control</span><strong>{number(results.ddpm[2].delta)} <small>pp · Easy − Shuffled</small></strong><span>95% CI [{number(results.ddpm[2].ci[0])}, {number(results.ddpm[2].ci[1])}] · paired p = {results.ddpm[2].p.toFixed(4)}</span></div></div></article>
<article class="case-study"><div class="case-copy"><span class="overline">03 · VAE / ImageNet-1K</span><h3>The operational gap<br/> persists at scale.</h3><p>Class-balanced VAE-hard selection at a 30% budget underperforms balanced Random by <strong>{number(-results.imagenet.best.delta,3)} percentage points</strong>.</p><p class="scope">ResNet-18 from scratch · 90 epochs · five matched seed pairs. Both arms use the same best-validation-Top-1 checkpoint rule.</p><p class="case-limit">95% paired CI [{number(results.imagenet.best.ci[0],3)}, {number(results.imagenet.best.ci[1],3)}] pp. This result is specific to this VAE, budget, selection policy, learner, and checkpoint protocol.</p><a class="source-link" href={source(results.sources.imagenet)}>Seed records &amp; provenance</a></div><div class="case-figure"><figure class="accuracy-chart" aria-label="Mean ImageNet-1K best-checkpoint Top-1 accuracy"><div class="plot-key"><span>Best-checkpoint Top-1</span><span>Accuracy, %</span></div>{[{label:'Proxy-Hard',value:results.imagenet.best.hardMean},{label:'Random',value:results.imagenet.best.randomMean}].map((r,i)=><div class="accuracy-row"><div><span>{r.label}</span><strong>{number(r.value,3)}%</strong></div><div class="accuracy-track"><span class={i===0?'hard-bar':'random-bar'} style={`width:${r.value/70*100}%`}></span></div></div>)}<div class="accuracy-axis"><span>0</span><span>35</span><span>70%</span></div><figcaption>Means from the five seed records. The bars show accuracy; the confidence interval below is for the paired difference.</figcaption></figure><div class="comparison-note"><span class="overline">Hard − Random</span><strong>{number(results.imagenet.best.delta,3)} <small>percentage points</small></strong><span>95% CI [{number(results.imagenet.best.ci[0],3)}, {number(results.imagenet.best.ci[1],3)}]</span></div><details class="seed-details"><summary>Endpoint and evaluation details</summary><p>VAE: stabilityai/sd-vae-ft-mse normalized-pixel reconstruction MSE. Select the largest scores within each training class; 384,300 training images per arm. Full 50,000-image validation is used for checkpoint selection and evaluation. Validation images do not enter the selection-score source.</p><p>The final-epoch-90 sensitivity endpoint gives {number(results.imagenet.final.delta,3)} pp, 95% CI [{number(results.imagenet.final.ci[0],3)}, {number(results.imagenet.final.ci[1],3)}].</p></details></div></article>
<aside class="positive-control"><span class="overline">A matched positive control</span><div><h3>Useful proxies are possible.</h3><p>Task-aligned ImageNet ResNet-18 CE → ResNet-50 CE passes all three gates for the declared retrieval/triage setting. At top 10%, it retrieves <strong>{number(results.positive.gate3[0].recall*100,2)}%</strong> of the target-hard samples versus 10% expected under Random.</p><p class="scope">This supports retrieval/triage within that setting. It does not establish generative-proxy validity or subset-retraining benefit.</p></div><a class="source-link" href={source(results.sources.positive)}>Control evidence</a></aside></section>
<section id="protocol" class="protocol section" aria-labelledby="protocol-title"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">04 / Evaluation protocol</span><h2 id="protocol-title">Audit a setting.<br/><em>Bound the claim.</em></h2><p>Declare the proxy and score direction, target, learner, splits, baselines, and downstream use before interpreting the evidence.</p></div><figure class="paper-figure"><a href={`${base}figures/cohe-protocol.svg`} aria-label="Open the original COHE protocol figure at full resolution"><img src={`${base}figures/cohe-protocol.svg`} alt="Original paper Figure 1: an audit tuple proceeds through dependence, held-out prediction, and operational transfer to a bounded claim tier." loading="lazy" width="905.54" height="133.55"/></a><figcaption>Original camera-ready Figure 1. Each gate licenses its own evidence level; the audit retains the scope of the final claim. <span class="figure-mobile-note">Scroll the figure to read its labels, or tap to open the full vector.</span></figcaption></figure><div class="protocol-notes"><div><h3>Calibrate, then freeze.</h3><p>Declare thresholds and safety margins using matched null behavior and variability. COHE uses setting-specific evidence requirements, not universal cutoffs.</p></div><div><h3>Keep targets distinct.</h3><p>First-learning and GradNorm relations remain target-specific. Gate 2 studies scalar or low-dimensional proxy inputs; it does not test all information in the full representations.</p></div></div><details class="original-figure"><summary>From the paper: held-out predictive validity</summary><figure class="paper-figure"><a href={`${base}figures/predictive-validity.svg`} aria-label="Open the original predictive validity figure at full resolution"><img src={`${base}figures/predictive-validity.svg`} alt="Original Figure 3 compares held-out R-squared for single and multi-proxy predictors; CE and margin stay near zero, while first-learning has a larger target-specific value. The shaded near-zero band is specific to this audit." loading="lazy" width="539.163375" height="203.372584"/></a><figcaption>Original camera-ready Figure 3, exported without numerical changes. The near-zero band is local to this audit; it is not a universal threshold. <span class="figure-mobile-note">Scroll the figure to read its labels, or tap to open the full vector.</span></figcaption></figure></details></div></section>
<section id="resources" class="resources container section" aria-labelledby="resources-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">05 / Paper &amp; resources</span><h2 id="resources-title">Read the paper.<br/><em>Inspect the evidence.</em></h2></div><div class="resources-layout"><div><h3 class="overline">Abstract</h3><p class="abstract">{results.abstract}</p></div><aside class="resource-links"><a href={paper}><strong>Paper</strong><span>Official NeurIPS 2026 record</span></a><a href={artifact}><strong>Code &amp; results</strong><span>Audit code and public numerical records</span></a><a href={release}><strong>Checkpoints</strong><span>Fixed v1.0-neurips2026 release</span></a><p class="resource-note">Reproduction coverage varies by experiment. DINOv2 selection endpoints were not retained; DDPM ordering releases 18 of 24 endpoints. Raw images and upstream weights are obtained separately.</p><a class="source-link" href={`${artifact}/blob/main/docs/REPRODUCIBILITY.md`}>Reproduction guide</a></aside></div><div id="citation" class="citation"><div><h3>Cite COHE</h3><button type="button" id="copy-bibtex" class="button">Copy BibTeX</button><span id="copy-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span></div><pre><code id="bibtex">{bibtex}</code></pre><p class="scope">Conference citation. No unverified DOI, volume, or proceedings identifier is included.</p></div></section></main>
<footer class="site-footer container"><a class="wordmark" href="#">COHE</a><span>Dayena Jeong &amp; Sunglok Choi · NeurIPS 2026</span><a href={artifact}>Scientific artifact</a></footer>
<script>
const button=document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('#copy-bibtex');button?.addEventListener('click',async()=>{const text=document.querySelector('#bibtex')?.textContent??'';const status=document.querySelector('#copy-status');try{await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);if(status)status.textContent='Copied to clipboard.';button.textContent='Copied';}catch{const range=document.createRange();range.selectNodeContents(document.querySelector('#bibtex')!);const selection=window.getSelection();selection?.removeAllRanges();selection?.addRange(range);if(status)status.textContent='Select and copy the highlighted citation.';}});
</script></body></html>
